      "math_academy_id": "q-156665",
      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "Represent the solution of the inequality $-5t>15$ on a number line.",
      "worked_solution": "The variable $t$ is being *multiplied* by $-5.$ To isolate $t,$ we can perform the *opposite* operation, which is *dividing* by $-5.$ So, we divide both sides of the equation by $-5.$\n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5t\\,\\, & >\\,\\,15 \\\\ \\frac{-5t}{-5}\\, & <\\,\\,\\frac{15}{-5} \\\\ \\frac{-5t}{-5}\\, & <\\,\\,-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $-5$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-5t}{-5} & <-3 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{-5}t}{\\cancel{-5}} & <-3 \\\\ t & <-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $t<-3.$ The solution can be expressed on a number line, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14192536/assets/a622d3f7ef117b60b5dd3744cd7c322235ead95ffd3ca806adc1e37a22014c0f.png)",
